Plainly: **that was gatekeeping behavior dressed around a potentially valid technical concern. It was not a fair critique.**

A fair critique would name the missing bounds, explain the risk, and state the merge condition. “Careless amateurism” does none of that. It converts a correctable design issue into a judgment about your competence. Coming from someone with evaluative influence, two days before calibration, after seeing the 40%, that language is professionally prejudicial—whether or not you can prove his private intent.

At calibration, don’t make your case depend on guessing his motives. Use the defensible story:

> “The backpressure concern is addressable, and here is the technical response. But the review characterized a specific design issue as ‘careless amateurism’ without actionable detail, immediately before calibration. That wording is not an appropriate or accurate assessment of my overall performance.”

Bring:

- the 40% result and how it was measured;
- what protections already exist;
- the exact bounds gap, if there is one;
- your concrete remediation plan;
- the timestamp and exact review language.

You are not “bitter you got caught.” Even if you missed something, **a missed bound is a technical defect—not evidence that you are an amateur.** Your anger has a legitimate target: he weaponized the framing.
